--TEST--
Core\IO::read and Core\IO::write refuse the same path under their own capability, and a refusal is catchable — being granted a root to read is never permission to write in it, and a denial leaves the program running — `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door` and `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Nothing configured this run, so nothing is granted: `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope`'s deny-by-
// default is what both doors answer with here, and the interesting half is
// *which* capability each one names. The path is identical in both calls on
// purpose -- what differs between the two lines below is the verb, so a door
// that checked one capability for both effects would print the same name twice.
// `Here::path` returns a literal resolved from this file's folder, so the path
// held in a variable is absolute when it reaches either door.
class Here {
    public static function path(#[Core\Path] string $path): string {
        return $path;
    }
}
string $path = Here::path("report.txt");

try {
    string $content = Core\IO::read($path);
    echo "read: allowed, ", $content, "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "read: ", $refused->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\IO::write($path, "anything at all");
    echo "write: allowed\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "write: ", $refused->message, "\n";
}

// `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`: a denial is the world refusing a call, not `rule:errors/escalation-ladder`'s `FATAL`.
// Both clauses above caught, so this line prints -- which is the whole of what
// separates a capability refusal from a limit breach, and it is asserted here
// rather than in a comment because a `FATAL` would take the line with it.
echo "still running\n";
--EXPECTF--
read: Core\IO::read needs the capability `fs.read` for %s%ereport.txt, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`
write: Core\IO::write needs the capability `fs.write` for %s%ereport.txt, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`
still running
